{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794126880,"nonce":"BkFfDrVRcB82fM45Rgr6PSnLgxM=","title":"Theme token overrides and :root overrides disagree: Rust reads never see :root, and spacing and radius overrides skip the CSS variables","message":"Found while checking how the book's theming chapter should describe overrides. Each row is what a reader sees after one override; probed with the Hybrid light theme on main.\n\n- :root { --text-primary: red } in app CSS: CSS rules read red. ThemeState::color(TextPrimary) and every widget default built from it (TextAreaConfig::default and the other layout and cn Rust defaults) still read the theme's value. ThemeState never consults the stylesheet's :root variables. Rust can read one only through active_stylesheet().get_variable(name), which returns the raw string (the cn time picker does this for its row height).\n- set_color_override(TextPrimary, blue): Rust reads see blue and the CSS variable map carries blue, so CSS rules see it too. A :root value still wins in CSS, so Rust and CSS then disagree.\n- set_radius_override and set_spacing_override: Rust reads of radius(token) and spacing_value(token) see the override, but to_css_variable_map still emits the theme's --radius-* and --space-* values, and the struct accessors radii() and spacing() ignore overrides too. The book's own examples use radii().radius_lg and spacing().space_4, so they ignore these overrides.\n- Typography and shadows have no override API.\n\nRough footprint: about 160 sites in blinc_layout and blinc_cn read the theme from Rust.\n\nOptions: make the CSS map and the struct accessors apply spacing and radius overrides; decide whether :root values for theme tokens should be mirrored into ThemeState on stylesheet load (one source of truth), or whether CSS is the documented route and Rust defaults stay on the theme.","files":null}]}